A function that calls its func-typed parameter names the parameter as the
call's callee rather than as an argument, and inlining substitutes the
argument there too. It did not: every walk in the inliner read a call's
receiver and arguments and skipped its callee, so `call(hello)` came out as
`f()`, naming a parameter of a function nobody emitted.

deny * `\bf\(\)` -- the parameter is gone once the call is inlined

-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

const func call(f func() string) => f()

const func hello() string {
    return "x"
}

const func usesIt() => call(hello)

var w = usesIt()

ui.window(title="t") {
    ui.text(value="got {w}")
}

output {
    none {
        html()
    }
}
